// What `main` returns is a process exit status, and a status is an int32.
//
// Any other width was accepted and then had to be made to fit. Natively it was
// truncated; on wasm the host reads the export's result back as the status, and
// an `int64` arrives there as a BigInt that cannot be one -- so the same
// program exited 0 natively and 1 on wasm. The suite differential is what said
// so, on a test written to check something else.
//
// `void` stays legal: it says the program has no status of its own to report,
// which is a real thing to say and is what most of the corpus says. That half
// is tests/main-signatures.abs.
//
// This joins the rules `main` already has -- no parameters, no overloads, not
// exported -- and for the same reason as the first of them: a declaration that
// cannot be honoured is refused rather than given a second, quieter meaning.

int64 main() {
    println("a status this wide is not a status");
    return 0;
}
